What is the output of the following Java program implementing an eager thread-safe Singleton?
```java
class SystemClock {
    private static final SystemClock INSTANCE = new SystemClock();
    private int tick = 0;
    private SystemClock() {} 
    public static SystemClock getInstance() { return INSTANCE; }
    public void step() { tick++; }
    public int getTick() { return tick; }
}
public class ClockApp {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        SystemClock c1 = SystemClock.getInstance();
        c1.step();
        SystemClock c2 = SystemClock.getInstance();
        c2.step();
        System.out.println(c1.getTick() + " " + (c1 == c2));
    }
}
```

What is the output of the following Java program implementing the Builder pattern for immutable objects?
```java
class RequestConfig {
    private final String url;
    private final int timeout;
    private RequestConfig(Builder b) {
        this.url = b.url;
        this.timeout = b.timeout;
    }
    public static class Builder {
        private String url = "localhost";
        private int timeout = 1000;
        public Builder url(String u) { this.url = u; return this; }
        public Builder timeout(int t) { this.timeout = t; return this; }
        public RequestConfig build() { return new RequestConfig(this); }
    }
    public String toString() { return url + ":" + timeout; }
}
public class BuilderApp {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        RequestConfig cfg = new RequestConfig.Builder().url("api.io").timeout(5000).build();
        System.out.println(cfg);
    }
}
```

What concurrency flaw exists in the following Double-Checked Locking Singleton implementation?
```java
class ConnectionPool {
    private static ConnectionPool instance;
    private ConnectionPool() {}
    public static ConnectionPool getInstance() {
        if (instance == null) {
            synchronized (ConnectionPool.class) {
                if (instance == null) {
                    instance = new ConnectionPool();
                }
            }
        }
        return instance;
    }
}
